# Why model at all - and which model shape fits which problem?
next: {"title":"Building a Markov model: states, cycles, transitions and the long tail","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/building-a-markov-model-states-cycles.cast"}

## The Missing Thirty-Seven Years
voice: male
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,40],"y":[0,100]}

A: Over these four parts we ask why health economists build models at all, and which shape of model fits which problem — and the answer starts with a decision someone had to make on evidence that ran out early.
    axes ax x_label "Age (years)" y_label "Surviving (%)"

B: Nusinersen, for spinal muscular atrophy. Untreated, the infantile form is brutal — most children die or need permanent ventilation within a couple of years.
    curve c_untreated_trial #b5482e expr 100*exp(-0.5*x) x_from 0 x_to 3
    label label_untreated "Natural history, no treatment" below-left #b5482e attach_to c_untreated_trial

    pause 0.3

A: With the drug the trial saw something real: children alive, and hitting motor milestones nobody expected them to hit.
    curve c_treated_trial #2f6b8f expr 100*exp(-0.09*x) x_from 0 x_to 3
    label label_treated "Nusinersen, trial data" above #2f6b8f attach_to c_treated_trial

B: That thin green sliver is the benefit somebody actually measured, with patients, under a protocol.
    region gain_trial x_from 0 x_to 3 between ["c_treated_trial","c_untreated_trial"] style.fill #87a878
    label label_observed "Measured: 3 years" below #87a878 attach_to gain_trial

A: And here the evidence simply stops. Three years, and the data runs out.
    arrow trial_line dashed #8f887c from.x 3 from.y 0 to.x 3 to.y 100
    label label_trial "Trial ends" right #8f887c attach_to trial_line
    delivery grave

    pause 0.5

B: The child doesn't stop at three, though. If the drug does what we hope, she's still out here at forty.
    point at.x 22 at.y 55 gesture circle

A: So counting only the sliver isn't the cautious choice — it quietly assumes the benefit ends the day the trial ends.
    highlight gain_trial effect glow

    pause 0.3

B: Extrapolating the untreated arm barely needs a model — it's already on the floor.
    curve c_untreated_ext dashed #b5482e expr 100*exp(-0.5*x) x_from 3 x_to 40

A: The treated line is the whole argument: assume the effect holds, and it drifts down gently for another thirty-seven years.
    curve c_treated_ext dashed #2f6b8f expr 76*exp(-0.02*(x-3)) x_from 3 x_to 40
    label label_hold "If the effect holds" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to c_treated_ext

B: Or assume it wanes — same drug, same three years of data, a very different life.
    curve c_treated_pess dashed #d0865f expr 76*exp(-0.06*(x-3)) x_from 3 x_to 40
    label label_wane "If the effect wanes" below-right #d0865f attach_to c_treated_pess

A: Everything between those lines is QALYs, and nearly all of it lies past the last observation.
    region gain_model x_from 3 x_to 40 between ["c_treated_ext","c_untreated_ext"] style.fill #f2c14e
    label label_modelled "Modelled: 37 years" above #3d3833 attach_to gain_model

B: Three years of data doing the work of forty years of life — quite the overtime shift for one dataset.
    highlight gain_model effect glow

    pause 0.3

A: Discounting at four percent softens the far end — a QALY at forty is worth about a fifth of one today — but softened is not deleted.
    point at.x 34 at.y 25 gesture tap

B: When Beslutningsforum said no in 2017 and yes in 2018 with an age limit, the health they were arguing over was almost entirely out here, in the dashed part.
    highlight c_treated_ext c_treated_pess effect pulse

A: So a model isn't evidence smuggled in — it's the assumption written down where someone can attack it.
    mark mark_model target label_modelled kind box
    delivery grave

B: You can now see the real question: not whether to extrapolate, but with which structure — and next time we ask when a simple tree is strong enough.

## When a Tree Is Enough
voice: male
level: advanced
use: decision_tree
with:
    root:
      id: choice
      type: decision
      label: Hip, age 68
      children: [{label: Replace, node: {id: hip, type: chance, label: Operate, children: [{label: Uncomplicated, node: {id: ok, type: terminal, label: Works, payoff: 8.5, cost: 120000}, probability: 0.92}, {label: Complication, probability: 0.08, node: {id: comp, type: terminal, label: Revision, payoff: 6.8, cost: 260000}}]}}, {label: Current care, node: {id: cons, type: terminal, label: Physio, payoff: 6.2, cost: 30000}}]

A: Now that we've seen the thirty-seven years the trial never watched, the obvious worry is that long horizons demand heavy machinery — so let's take a case where one square and two branches settle it.
    draw node_choice label_choice

B: Our patient is sixty-eight with an end-stage arthritic hip. Replace it — and the circle marks where chance takes over from us.
    draw edge_choice_hip branchlabel_choice_hip node_hip label_hip

A: Ninety-two out of a hundred go smoothly: eight and a half discounted QALYs over the remaining years, for about a hundred and twenty thousand kroner.
    draw edge_hip_ok branchlabel_hip_ok node_ok label_ok payoff_ok

    pause 0.3

B: The other eight percent get an infection or a revision — six point eight QALYs, and the cost more than doubles, to two hundred and sixty thousand.
    draw edge_hip_comp branchlabel_hip_comp node_comp label_comp payoff_comp

    pause 0.3

A: And current care as comparator: physiotherapy and painkillers, thirty thousand kroner, six point two QALYs — the arthritis simply stays.
    draw edge_choice_cons branchlabel_choice_cons node_cons label_cons payoff_cons

B: Everything right of that circle happens once, in one order, and is then finished with.
    point at.ref node_hip gesture circle

A: So we fold it back: nought point nine two times eight point five, plus nought point nought eight times six point eight — 8.36 QALYs, at an expected hundred and thirty-one thousand kroner.
    label ev_hip "E[E] = 8.36 QALYs · E[C] = kr 131 000" below #2f6b8f attach_to node_hip

B: Nothing to fold on the other side — six point two QALYs for thirty thousand, flat.
    label ev_cons "6.2 QALYs · kr 30 000" below #2f6b8f attach_to node_cons

A: Delta E of 2.16 QALYs, delta C of about a hundred and one thousand — an ICER near forty-seven thousand kroner per QALY.
    text icer_note "ICER ≈ kr 47 000 / QALY" #b5482e x 640 y 240 font_size 32
    mark mark_icer target icer_note kind box
    delivery grave

B: Spectacular value, arrived at with arithmetic you could do on a napkin — and precisely the sort of number that never reaches a front page, unlike our expensive friend nusinersen.
    highlight icer_note effect glow

    pause 0.5

    clear

A: So a tree suffices when the events happen once, in a fixed order.
    text t_title "A tree is enough when…" x 500 y 610 font_size 34
    text t1 "1.  Events happen once, in a fixed order" x 500 y 510 font_size 27

B: When no state can be re-entered — a replaced hip does not quietly go back to being arthritic on a Tuesday.
    text t2 "2.  No state can be re-entered" x 500 y 445 font_size 27

A: And when everything after the last branch collapses into one number sitting at the tip.
    text t3 "3.  The rest of life sums into one number at each tip" x 500 y 380 font_size 27

B: Which exposes the thing people get backwards: that tree cheerfully carried forty years of life. The horizon was never what defeated it.
    text punch "Length is not the problem. Repetition is." #b5482e x 500 y 250 font_size 30
    mark punch_box target punch kind box
    delivery grave

A: Repetition is — the moment a patient can fall ill, recover and fall ill again, the tree sprouts branches faster than she can live them, and that is where we go next.
    highlight punch effect pulse

## When Disease Loops Back
voice: male
level: advanced
use: markov_model
with:
    title: 'SMA on nusinersen: one year at a time'
    states:
      - No milestones
      - Sits
      - Dead
    transitions:
      - {from: No milestones, to: Sits, label: '0.20'}
      - {from: Sits, to: No milestones, label: '0.05'}
      - {from: No milestones, to: Dead, label: '0.10'}
      - {from: Sits, to: Dead, label: '0.02'}
    self_loops:
      - {state: No milestones, label: '0.70'}
      - {state: Sits, label: '0.93'}
    layout: chain

A: Now that we have seen a tree handle a one-shot choice, watch what happens when the disease refuses to stay where the tree put it.
    draw title state_no_milestones state_label_no_milestones

B: Our child with spinal muscular atrophy on nusinersen: down here, no motor milestones; up here, sitting unsupported.
    draw state_sits state_label_sits

A: And the third state, which is the one guest the hotel never checks out of.
    draw state_dead state_label_dead

    pause 0.3

B: In a year, twenty percent of children in the lower state gain sitting — that arrow upward is the drug's entire claim.
    draw t_0 t_label_0

A: But five percent slide back down, and a decision tree simply cannot draw that arrow; its branches only ever point forward.
    draw t_1 t_label_1

B: Death takes ten percent from the worse state each year, two percent from the better one.
    draw t_2 t_label_2 t_3 t_label_3

A: And the rest just stay put — the self-loops, which quietly hold most of the probability.
    draw loop_no_milestones loop_label_no_milestones loop_sits loop_label_sits

B: Dead is absorbing: arrows in, none out — that's what lets the model run for forty years and still stop honestly.
    highlight state_dead state_label_dead effect glow

    pause 0.5

    clear keep []

A: Strip the picture away, and every arrow you just drew is only a number in a table.
    text p_head "P — one-year transition probabilities" x 500 y 650 font_size 32

B: Rows are where you are now, columns where you are one cycle later — and the cycle here is one year.
    text h_no "to: No milestones" #8f887c x 470 y 580 font_size 22
    text h_sits "to: Sits" #8f887c x 650 y 580 font_size 22
    text h_dead "to: Dead" #8f887c x 800 y 580 font_size 22
    text rl_no "No milestones" x 250 y 510 font_size 24
    text rl_sits "Sits" x 250 y 440 font_size 24
    text rl_dead "Dead" x 250 y 370 font_size 24

A: From no milestones: seventy percent stay, twenty gain sitting, ten die.
    text c00 "0.70" x 470 y 510 font_size 28
    text c01 "0.20" #2f6b8f x 650 y 510 font_size 28
    text c02 "0.10" #b5482e x 800 y 510 font_size 28

B: From sitting: ninety-three stay, five slip back, two die.
    text c10 "0.05" #2f6b8f x 470 y 440 font_size 28
    text c11 "0.93" x 650 y 440 font_size 28
    text c12 "0.02" #b5482e x 800 y 440 font_size 28

A: Dead stays dead — and every row sums to one, because nobody falls out of the model.
    text c20 "0" x 470 y 370 font_size 28
    text c21 "0" x 650 y 370 font_size 28
    text c22 "1" x 800 y 370 font_size 28
    text note_rows "Every row sums to 1 — everyone is somewhere, every year" #8f887c x 500 y 300 font_size 24

    pause 0.4

B: Multiply the cohort by P forty times and you have the forty-year life nobody ever watched.
    text note_tail "Trial: 3 cycles observed  ·  Model: 40 cycles  ·  37 come from P" x 500 y 215 font_size 28
    mark mark_tail target note_tail kind box

A: Three cycles came from the trial, thirty-seven from this table — which is why the nusinersen ICER was driven less by the data than by the tail P generated.
    highlight note_tail effect glow delivery grave

B: So a tree when the story ends, a Markov when it recurs — and the price of the loop is that most of your answer now lives in P.
    text synth "If the disease loops back, the model must loop back too" #2f6b8f x 500 y 120 font_size 30

## Choosing the Simplest Model That Survives Scrutiny
voice: male
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,100]}

    axes ax x_label "Realism: how much patient history the model carries" y_label Transparency hidden true
    dot p_tree #2f6b8f at.x 18 at.y 78 hidden true
    label l_tree "Decision tree" above #2f6b8f attach_to p_tree hidden true
    label note_tree "One-off event, short horizon" below-right attach_to p_tree hidden true
    dot p_markov #2f6b8f at.x 50 at.y 50 hidden true
    label l_markov "Markov model" above #2f6b8f attach_to p_markov hidden true
    mark mark_markov target l_markov kind box hidden true
    label note_markov "States and cycles, no memory" below-right attach_to p_markov hidden true
    dot p_des #2f6b8f at.x 82 at.y 20 hidden true
    label l_des "DES / individual-level" above #2f6b8f attach_to p_des hidden true
    label note_des "Queues, capacity, full histories" below-left attach_to p_des hidden true
    arrow a_tm p_tree -> p_markov curved #8f887c hidden true
    arrow a_md p_markov -> p_des curved #8f887c hidden true

A: Now that we've seen how disease loops back on itself, the last question is the practical one: which model shape do you reach for, and what does each one cost you?
    draw ax

B: First rung, and the most transparent object we own: the decision tree. Spinraza or standard care, a branch, a probability, a payoff.
    draw p_tree l_tree

A: Which is genuinely enough when the story happens once inside the trial window — an operation, a scan result, a single outcome.
    draw note_tree

    pause 0.3

B: But our patient lives forty years and the trial ran three. Ask a tree to handle one event a year for forty years and it needs two to the fortieth branches —
    draw p_markov a_tm l_markov

A: — one point one trillion of them. Nobody is drawing that whiteboard.
    point at.ref p_tree gesture tap

A: The Markov model folds that trillion back into a handful of states and one transition matrix, applied cycle after cycle across the missing thirty-seven years.
    draw note_markov

B: And what you hand over for that is memory — a state doesn't know whether this is your first relapse or your fourth; everyone standing in it gets the same future.
    focus p_markov l_markov note_markov

    pause 0.3

A: So you climb again: discrete event simulation, or individual-level models, where every simulated patient carries their own history and their own clock.
    draw p_des a_md l_des

B: Which is what you need when the answer depends on the queue — if the country has one intrathecal injection room and it's booked until Thursday, no state diagram will ever tell you that.
    draw note_des

A: Borrowed technology, incidentally — discrete event simulation came out of operations research, modelling factory floors and airport queues long before anyone pointed it at a hospital.
    highlight p_des l_des effect glow

B: And here is the bill for climbing: all three rungs sit on one falling line — every step right buys realism and spends transparency.
    curve transparency_line dashed #8f887c expr "92 - 0.8*x" x_from 8 x_to 88
    label label_transp "Transparency falls as realism rises" right #8f887c attach_to transparency_line

A: Down here you're running thousands of patients, re-running them for every parameter draw, and handing Beslutningsforum a model that maybe three people in the room can actually check.
    focus p_des note_des transparency_line delivery grave

    pause 0.4

B: Right — strip it back to the choosing rule.
    clear keep ["ax","transparency_line","label_transp","p_tree","l_tree","p_markov","l_markov","p_des","l_des"] delivery brisk

A: Climb one rung only when the question forces you: when time, recurrence, queues or individual histories would otherwise change the ICER.
    text rule_text "Climb a rung only when the question forces you." #b5482e x 500 y 700 font_size 30

B: For a chronic disease over a forty-year horizon, discounted at four percent, that lands you squarely on the middle rung — which is exactly what we build next time: states, cycles, transitions.
    draw mark_markov

A: Across all four parts, then: a model isn't a claim about reality, it's the cheapest structure that carries a three-year trial past its last follow-up without lying — and the simplest one that survives scrutiny wins.
    highlight rule_text effect pulse

## Where next
